The main reason you should use SVN over CVS is that SVN has atomic
commits, so you won't wreck things because two people accidentally
commited at the same time. Also, I hear branch management is much
easier in SVN.

Git, mercurial and Bazaar are distributed systems, like bitkeeper.
Branching is ultra-easy with these systems, and you get the benefit of
revision control even on your own local copy, because each copy is
also a repository. You can then push changesets back and forth between
your friends before (optionally) sending them to a central server for
the "official" version. This feature is what allowed the Linux kernel
maintainers to have similar, but not identical trees, with a minimum
of fuss.

As to where, they are all open source, so just google them.




